The development of the \citev{trame-radial-menu} more or less
followed the steps of the agile methodology \cite{agile}, with planning,
analysis, design, implementation, testing, deployment, and start over.

There were 3 people from the team being the product owners, a developer and a
code reviewer.

\subsubsection{Analysis}

What was asked in a first place was a "krita palette" for trame-slicer.

The krita palette has a big variety of features and shows as a radial buttons
around the mouse click \cref{fig:krita-palette}. It has a side menu to the right
for the brush options, and it makes it easy for the user to quickly switch
between different drawing tools. Trame slicer's medical viewer can benefit from
the same interface.

\insertfigure
    {assets/images/krita-palette}
    {height=0.3\textheight}
    {Krita palette \cite{Krita}}
    {fig:krita-palette}

Trame slicer's medical viewer almost has the same tools and right now these
tools are in a side menu as shown in \cref{fig:medical-viewer-ui}

\insertfigure
    {assets/images/medical-viewer-ui}
    {height=0.3\textheight}
    {Medical Viewer UI}
    {fig:medical-viewer-ui}

First step was to establish a state of the art \cref{appendix:sota-pie-menu}.

It was about whether some solution already exists, what are the features, design
choices, technologies used, licence.

Almost all the items of the start of the art had poor maintenance, some used a
framework that was too big or incompatible with trame-slicer, and none had the
Vue modularity and all the features that were needed.

No item of the state of the art fitted the expectations, it was decided to
develop our own radial menu, with design choices and technology choices inspired
from the items of the state of the art.

\subsubsection{Development}

Then was the phase of development of the radial menu in VueJS. A first POC
\cref{fig:first-poc-radial-menu} was shown to the product owners with its API
\cref{lst:first-poc}, their feedback refined the design, the API and the
features they wanted. 

In this first POC the API \cref{lst:first-poc} made it possible to put multiple
wheels, an outer RadWheel would be put as a child component of the inner
RadWheel. The major problem was that the kind of RadItem we can put was very
limited (only to text and checkboxes). The product owners want to be able to put
any HTML element.

