\subsubsection{Maintenance process}

Finally when everything was ready, the project was put on a Kitware owned
repository and published to PyPI through a GitHub workflow
\cite{github-actions}.

Now when new features are asked, they are added to the issues, I make a PR which
is also reviewed by the Code Reviewer and then merged, like
\cref{pr:Kitware/trame-radial-menu/1}.

The project was published to PyPI so it is importable with pip install
trame-radial-menu, it was added to the trame widget list and announced in the
trame news with \cref{pr:Kitware/trame/895} and \cref{pr:Kitware/trame/897}.

\subsubsection{\refrepo{trame-slicer} medical viewer example}

As the use cases imagined for the radial menu were softwares like trame-slicer's
medical viewer, an integration of the \citev{trame-radial-menu} in this app was
expected.

The Medical Viewer gives  a good example of a complex app with complex UI and
Logic. This is what this section is about.

\Cref{fig:medical-viewer-classes} shows us a simplified view of the classes
used. These classes are singleton (see \cite{design_patterns}) which means
that only one object is of each class. An arrow between SegmentEditorUI and
MedicalViewUI means that the MedicalViewerUI instantiates and knows the
SegmentEditorUI. 

\insertfigure{assets/images/medical-viewer-1}{width=\textwidth}{Simplified
Medical Viewer's class diagram. These classes are singletons and arrows aren't
inheritance but mean "is known by"}{fig:medical-viewer-classes}

The UI part and the Logic part have two separate responsabilities, the UI
classes handle the interface with the user (through trame UI elements) and
stores the states of the UI (through typed states \cref{sec:typedstates}).  The
Logic part is connected to the UI parts' triggers (functions that the user can
trigger through the UI) and handles the interface with Slicer. If a user clicks
a button to change tool the \texttt{SegmentEditorUI.set\_active\_tool("Brush")}
is called, which calls a corresponding procedure in the SegmentEditorLogic which
will tell the Slicer instance to change tool.

In that manner, the whole UI is built like a tree just like HTML. These nodes
correspond to UI parts and have their own typed state (to know which
segmentation tool is selected for example). The exact same structure as the UI
is used for the logic part. Every logic object has a \texttt{set\_ui} property
that expects the corresponding UI as parameters. It registers the UI, connects
their functions (through Signals in the
\definerepo{https://github.com/KitwareMedical/py-undo-stack}{py-undo-stack}
repo) so that UI classes's triggers trigger the right functions in the Logic
class.

\subsubsection{Radial Menu in the Medical Viewer example}

Trame-slicer was forked in a personnal branch to integrate the Radial Menu in
the Medical Viewer, see \cref{repo:trame-slicer/radial-menu}.

Integrating the Radial Menu in the UI, adding buttons as RadItems and set the
side menu was quite straight forward. But the right menu opened on each right
click which this wasn't a desired behavious when the right click was bind to
other tasks like zooming or stop adding markups (annotations). Fixing this
required catching the right click event not from the Javascript but from the
Slicer instance itself. This required diving in the architecture of Slicer
itself, the part of the architecture which handles the right click is shown in
two different cases: when the user right clicks in the void of the scene
\cref{fig:slicer-architecture-void}, and when the user right clicks on a
specific node \cref{fig:slicer-architecture-node}.

\insertfigure{assets/images/slicer-architecture-1}{width=\textwidth}{Propagation
of a right click in the void of a view in 3D Slicer through 3D Slicer's
architecture}{fig:slicer-architecture-void}

In the first case the right click must be intercepted at the level of the
\texttt{vtkMRMLApplicationLogic} as before it there could still be some
processing to decide whether the click is valid or not, and after that we dive
into handling the \citev{Qt} elements interaction. In the second case the events
must be caught at the \texttt{vtkMRMLDisplayNode}

\insertfigure{assets/images/slicer-architecture-2}{width=\textwidth}{Propagation
of a right click on a node of a view in 3D Slicer through 3D Slicer's
architecture}{fig:slicer-architecture-node}

In the first case the right click now makes the tool radial menu open, and in
the second case the logic store the Slicer markup node and opens a context menu
(which is another radial menu with nothing except a side menu open) to have
actions depending on the markup node type. For example for Fiducial nodes
(annotation points in the scene), the delete, select and unselect options are
shown.

In the end \cref{fig:medical-viewer-w-radial-menu} shows the radial menu
integrated to trame-slicer, making it possible to easily select different markup
nodes or segmentation tools, change tools' parameters or interact with markup
noes on the views.

\insertfigure{assets/images/trame-slicer-w-radial-menu}{height=0.3\textheight}{Radial Menu integration into trame-slicer's Medical Viewer}{fig:medical-viewer-w-radial-menu}